00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:04.870 align:start position:0%
as the supreme court appears poised to  overturn roe v wade treasury secretary  janet yellen testified to the senate  that restricting access to abortion  would have very damaging effects on the  economy yellen's key argument is that  access to abortion allows more women to  participate in the workforce and get an  education which increases their earning  potential  for example after abortion restrictions  were lifted following roe v wade in 1973  black women's participation in the labor  force increased by 6.9 percentage points  however among white women it only  increased by two points  yellen also noted abortion access helps  keep more people out of poverty and in  need of public assistance  a decade-long study known as the  turnaway study tracked 1 000 women some  of whom obtained abortions and some were  turned away from getting one  the study found the ones who were denied  were more likely to experience household  poverty  be unable to cover basic living expenses  have a lower credit score  more debt  and more exposure to the possibility of  bankruptcy and eviction  all of this also impacts the economy as  a whole  state level abortion restrictions cost  state economies a combined 105 billion  dollars per year  due to reduced labor force participation  and earning levels as well as more time  off and turnover republican senator tim  scott of south carolina hit back at  yelen's comments as harsh and unusually  piercing adding i think framing abortion  in the context of labor force  participation just feels callous to me  the senator argued more focus should be  placed on child tax credits child  infrastructure  and other measures to support families  if roe v wade is overturned it could  lead to 26 states banning abortion  according to the pro-choice guttmacher  institute  texas now bans all abortions after six  weeks of pregnancy  and florida and arizona have enacted  their own 15-week bans in advance of the  court's filing  in oklahoma house lawmakers gave final  approval to a bill banning abortion on  may 19th the bill bans abortions  starting from the moment of  fertilization  exceptions can be made for medical  emergencies rape incest or sexual  assault but only if those incidents have  been reported to law enforcement  the ban would be enforced through  private citizens lawsuits  the bill will now go to republican  governor kevin stitt who opposes  abortion rights and is expected to sign  it into law which would make it the  strictest measures against the procedure  in the u.s
 

